If you're building anything on the side — a business, a habit, a version of your life that looks different from the one you have now — you already know the real problem isn't motivation. It's that everything lives in a different app, a different notebook, a different tab you swore you'd get back to.

The apps below aren't about adding more tracking to your day. They're about picking the *right* few, so you stop being the one holding it all together in your head.

## 5 apps worth actually setting up

**1. Notion, for one place to put everything.** If you're currently splitting your life across a notes app, a spreadsheet, and a physical planner, Notion lets you build one connected system instead — goals, habits, business numbers, ideas you don't want to lose. The learning curve is real for the first hour. After that, it's the thing you open instead of five other apps.

**2. Base44, if you've ever thought "I wish there was an app for this."** Most of us have a specific little tracking problem that doesn't quite fit any app in the store — a weird combination of habit + budget + project tracker, or something built around exactly how you work. Base44 lets you describe what you want in plain language and get a working app back, no coding required. It's the closest thing to "just build the tool yourself" without actually needing to be a developer.

**3. Google Calendar, if tracking your life means tracking someone else's too.** Whether it's a partner, a co-founder, or family you're coordinating with, half of feeling "on top of things" is just not double-booking yourself. It's not glamorous, but a shared calendar both people actually check beats any fancier app neither of you opens.

**4. Zapier, so the boring stuff runs without you.** It quietly connects the apps you already use — so a new client inquiry logs itself, a receipt gets filed, a weekly report builds itself before you sit down. The upfront setup takes an afternoon; after that, it's work you never have to remember to do again.

**5. YNAB (You Need A Budget), for the "business" half of your life and business.** You don't need anything complicated here — just something that shows you, at a glance, what's actually coming in and going out. YNAB's whole approach is built around giving every dollar a job before it disappears, which is a genuinely different way to see your own numbers. The goal isn't a perfect financial system. It's not being surprised by them.

If you want something to read while you build this out: **Building a Second Brain** by Tiago Forte — a genuinely useful framework for deciding what deserves a system and what doesn't. [Building a Second Brain, Tiago Forte](https://link.amazon/B0hvI7yX0)

## The reminder

You don't need five new apps by Friday. ==Pick the one thing you're currently tracking in your head that shouldn't be there, and give it one real home this week.== That's the whole system — everything else is just maintenance.

